Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||11-08-2000||04:23 PM||||i recently went to the muffler shop because i suspected my cat was going bad, and turned out it was. don't have the money right now to replace it, so i was gonna use a test pipe- i have heard of people without converters being able to shoot flames, should i be able to? also, i have a crack in my exhaust manifolds, will this hurt it? and lastly, will it still shoot flames with a muffler? 2.8 v6, flowmaster (cool muffler)<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460599&p=25516673"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||11-08-2000||06:24 PM||||No it wont hurt it, no it wont shoot flames just by removing the cat. Alot of us dont have cat converters on our cars, and they dont shoot flames.||||reg||1
Z||000002||V8Fiero||11-08-2000||08:12 PM||||About $8 worth of straight pipe and your problems are solved. <P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||11-09-2000||03:18 AM||||I don't have a cat and my car shoots flames  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>and I still have a muffler, too.  <P>One of the reasons the cat is there is to burn any unburnt fuel that gets out of the engine, so when you take it off that fuel burns someplace else, which is usually when it mixes with enough oxygen at the exhaust tips.  That's my theory, anyways  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Smith 85 2M4||11-09-2000||01:38 PM||||It may or may not shoot flames, but your cracked exhaust manifolds can cause other problems.  The cracks will suck additional oxygen into your exhaust stream.  Your O2 sensor will sense the excess oxygen and produce a signal to tell the computer to provide more fuel to correct the fuel mixture.  Your engine will run too rich and cause your gas mileage to suffer and foul the spark plugs.  On the other hand, the extra fuel may cause the "shooting flames".  Keep in mind that fire and plastic bumpers don't mix too well.  I don't want to sound gripy, just some friendly advice.<P>------------------<BR>Smith 85 2M4||||reg||7
Z||000005||Standard||11-10-2000||02:39 AM||||Oh yeah.. I also have a front manifold that looks like it's cracked almost in half from what I can tell.  I still manage to average 20mpg in town and I've gotten a best of 27mpg going a constant 85-90mph... and I've only been pulled over twice 'cause my cars too loud   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I haven't noticed any adverse effects on the bumber, either.  That's why there's metal heat sheilds above the exhaust tips  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">        <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 11-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mattsfiero||11-10-2000||08:43 AM||||First Question Is why would you want to throw flames out the Exahust? Secound question is you say that the front Manifold is cracked almost in half? The front manifold Pipe is your exahust manifold. You have a single in Dual out exahust and the only other manifold would be your intake. I am assuming this is a 2.8. There could be some very severe affects by driving the car for any long period of time like this and would suggest you getting to the local salvage yard and finding a good replacement. (Cheapest solution) before you start getting any problems.<P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@excite.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||4
Z||000007||Master_Sushi||11-10-2000||11:10 AM||||How do you take the cat off? Do you have to saws all it, or weld it off? Then just clam a pipe in? I think mine "might" be going out. I really dont want to spend much money right now either. <P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000008||Old Lar||11-10-2000||11:39 AM||||You can get a new cat for less than $100.  As for cutting the pipe, a saws all works, a cutting torch is faster.  Just don't burn up anything else whiile you are cutting.||||reg||3
Z||000009||WisconsinGT||11-10-2000||01:19 PM||||Well, this is getting interesting. For a long time now, I thought I had a rich mixture problem, but did not kow the cause of it. I do not have a cat on my car. (Bought it that way.) Anyway, I never put a cat on thinking that I would loose it quickly because I have a rich mixture. But in fact, the reson I have a rich mixture is due to the fact I do not have a cat. Makes sense to me. I even remember posting a question about this here at Pennocks. Some of you thought that if you have no cat you should not have a rich mixture. What's the real answer?  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Smith 85 2M4||11-10-2000||01:48 PM||||To WisconsinGT:<P>The catalytic converter shouldn't have any effect on fuel mixture.  The O2 sensor is upstream so it makes its reading in the exhaust stream before it ever reaches the catalytic converter.  Taking of the cat should not effect the accuracy of the O2 sensor.  Most very late model cars (1997 and up) have an O2 sensor before and after the cat (this system is called On Board Diagnostic 2, or OBD2).  This system will cause problems if the cat is removed.  I assure you there is no Fiero equipped with OBD2 unless it has received an engine from a newer donor car.  You should look elsewhere for the cause of the rich mixture.<P>------------------<BR>Smith 85 2M4||||reg||1
